    <owl:Ontology rdf:about="https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iec60050/instances">
        <owl:versionIRI rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iec60050/instances/1.0.0"/>
        <owl:imports rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iec60050/1.0.0"/>
        <terms:created rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-18</terms:created>
        <terms:creator>TODO: author name, ORCID</terms:creator>
        <terms:description xml:lang="en">Named individuals for the IEC 60050-351 ontology design pattern: the time and quality characteristics a model states about the behaviour of a control.

They are kept apart from the pattern so that terminology and vocabulary can be maintained separately, in the same arrangement as the quality of service parameters of the ISO 7498 pattern. A model importing only the pattern gets the classes; one importing this file gets the vocabulary as well.</terms:description>
        <terms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
        <terms:publisher>TODO: institution</terms:publisher>
        <terms:title xml:lang="en">IEC 60050-351 Pattern: Named Individuals</terms:title>
        <bibo:status xml:lang="en">Draft</bibo:status>
        <vann:preferredNamespacePrefix>iec60050-inst</vann:preferredNamespacePrefix>
        <vann:preferredNamespaceUri>https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iec60050/instances#</vann:preferredNamespaceUri>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every individual here carries the clause of DIN IEC 60050-351:2014 that defines it, and where the standard gives a formula symbol that symbol is recorded as well, so a model stays checkable against the figures of the standard.

Seven of the definitions are reconstructed from the German text. Their English wording is interrupted by formula figures in the printed standard, which the extraction could not resolve, and the note on each affected individual says so.</rdfs:comment>
        <owl:versionInfo>1.0.0</owl:versionInfo>
    </owl:Ontology>
    


